CONSPECTUS: Developments in strategies for the synthesis of oxo−hydroxo lanthanide $\mathrm{{\bar{(Ln)}}}$ clusters during the past few decades have resulted in abundant relevant crystalline materials that exhibit attractive structures and interesting properties. The combination of these oxo−hydroxo Ln clusters and organic ligands has led to the formation of new extended arrays of Ln cluster organic frameworks (LnCOFs). In contrast to metal−organic frameworks, the incorporation of particular characteristics of clusters provides the opportunity to develop performances not available in single-metal compounds. Even with steady advances in oxo−hydroxo Ln clusters, progress in LnCOFs is less developed.
To obtain LnCOFs, one premise is to induce the oxophilic Ln ions undergoing aggregation. Meanwhile, the organic ligands should have extra coordination sites for further expansion. Multidentate organic ligands like pyrazinecarboxylic acid and pyridinecarboxylic acid containing O and N donors will meet these two requirements. Their carboxyl groups will induce the aggregation of Ln ions, while the N donors can serve as potential extension sites. To make more open frameworks or if the oxo−hydroxo Ln clusters fail to be congregated or connected, then a second ligand is necessary. The introduction of the suitable second ligand may occupy a partial coordination sphere of Ln ions and ultimately benefit the connection process.
In this Account, we introduce the origin and evolution of the induced aggregation and synergistic coordination strategy. According to the attributes of the organic ligands in the documented LnCOFs, we classify them into linear and nonlinear groups in the second and third parts. From the aspect of ligand-induced aggregation, isonicotinic acid (HIN) and lengthened 4-(4- pyridyl)benzoic acid (HPBA) ligands as well as their nonlinear analogues are settled as typical models. From the aspect of synergistic coordination, chelating ligands like 1,2-benzenedicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(1,2–H_{2}B D C)}$ and acetic acid (HOAc) play significant roles. Moreover, three types of synergistic coordination are discussed in detail: synergistic coordination between two types of organic ligands, synergistic coordination between organic and inorganic ligands, and simultaneous synergistic coordination of aforementioned two types. From the aspect of LnCOF products, in addition to traditional pure LnCOFs, new types of heterometallic frameworks containing two types of cluster building units have been developed.
Although this Account focuses on the nuclearity and coordination aspects of LnCOFs, we anticipate that it will stimulate more efforts in the further study of their properties beyond the exploratory synthesis. More importantly, synergistic coordination may be applied to other systems and inspire crystal design and targeted assembly of new functional materials.
## 1. INTRODUCTION
Metal−oxo clusters $\left(\mathrm{MOCs}\right)^{1-3}$ and metal−organic frameworks $\left(\mathrm{MOFs}\right)^{4-6}$ represent two outstanding superstars in the recent development of crystalline materials. The significance of the combination of cluster chemistry and porous solids is revealed in both areas, as evidenced by the emerging research work on transition metal cluster organic frameworks (TMCOFs).7,8 From the structural aspect, TMCOFs merge the characteristics of both transition metal−oxo clusters (TMOCs) and transition metal−organic frameworks (TMOFs). In contrast to TMOCs, TMCOFs are extended arrays with higher stabilities, while compared with TMOFs,
TMCOFs are higher-nuclearity TMOC architectures, where only partial central atoms in high-nuclearity clusters participate in the coordination with organic ligands. Except that the porous nature of the frameworks is reserved, particular characteristics of the clusters can be incorporated into the obtained frameworks in the aspect of material performance. As a result, unprecedented architectures and excellent properties related to the structures are achieved. According to the attributes of the metal ions, a broad range of MCOFs based on diverse MOCs from monovalent, divalent, and trivalent lanthanides (Ln), tetravalent titanium, and high-valent tungsten and molybdenum have been reported.9−14
To date, a common synthetic approach for making LnCOFs is to control the hydrolysis of $\operatorname{Ln}(\operatorname{III})$ salts at relatively high pH values. On the contrary, we developed a new synthetic route for LnCOFs by using Ln oxides rather than $\operatorname{Ln}(\operatorname{III})$ salts as the source of lanthanides at relatively low $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ . Thanks to the diverse synthetic strategy, research on LnCOFs is undergoing encouraging progress. To date, a series of LnCOFs based on a variety of Ln second building units (SBUs) ranging from low nuclearity $(\mathrm{Ln}_{2},\ \mathrm{Ln}_{4},\ \mathrm{Ln}_{6},$ or $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{Ln}_{7}.}\end{array}$ ) to high nuclearity $\mathrm{{(Ln_{10},}}$ $\mathrm{Ln_{12},\ L n_{14},\ L n_{26},\ L n_{36},}$ or $\mathrm{Ln}_{48}\mathrm{.}$ ) have been reported. With respect to the connectors, multi-O-donor linkers, either inorganic or organic, are preferred because of the oxophilic nature of the Ln ions. Among them, there is one type of multifunctional bridge ligand where both O donors and N donors are simultaneously included, such as aspartic acid and 2,2′-dipyridine-4,4′-dicarboxylate.17,18
Besides pure LnCOFs, there is great potential to construct heterometallic frameworks on the basis of the hard−soft acid/ base theory (Scheme 1). The different affinities of Ln and TM ions toward different donors may induce their respective aggregation along with the generation of heterometallic frameworks. To further investigate the impact of ligands on the assembly process and to understand the formation mechanism of LnCOFs, we introduced a second ligand.19 The unique synergy between the main ligand and the second ligand suggests that “synergistic coordination” is a useful synthetic strategy in the design and synthesis of LnCOFs. In order to explore synergistic coordination between different systems of ligands, like organic−organic or organic−inorganic synergistic coordination, we can increase the distance between the O donors and N donors. Alternatively, we can also change the angle of the two types of donors. More exciting is the fact that the strategies of induced aggregation and synergistic coordination are extensively approved and widely used. In addition, their potential applications have provided us an unabated impetus for continuous research. In this Account, the highlights of our efforts on the construction of LnCOFs by this strategy are summarized. Moreover, linear ligands, such as isonicotinic acid (HIN) and lengthened 4-(4-pyridyl)benzoic acid (HPBA), as well as nonlinear ligands are set as typical models. Finally, future perspectives for development will be given.
## 2. INDUCED AGGREGATION AND SYNERGISTIC COORDINATION BY LINEAR LIGANDS
## 2.1. Induced Aggregation by Isonicotinic Acid
Why are linear ligands used? The reason is that they reduce steric hindrance, serve as pillars, and prevent the aggregation of hybrid clusters. Why is HIN used as a ligand? On one hand, it is a rigid ligand with oxygen and nitrogen donors on opposite sides, enabling the IN ligand to act as a linear bridge. On the other hand, the carboxyl group may induce the oxophilic Ln ions to undergo aggregation of oxo−hydroxo Ln clusters while the nitrogen atoms can coordinate to TM ions, and thus, extended solids containing oxo−hydroxo Ln cores and TM ions might be obtained. Why are copper halides used as the source of TM ions? Copper halide aggregates represent a versatile family in coordination chemistry. What is more, the N donors in HIN ligands have strong affinity toward these connectors. Thus, they can exist as connectors (discrete motifs or copper halide-based building blocks) embedded in coordination polymers.
By HIN-ligand-induced synthesis in the $\mathrm{Ln}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ plus copper halide hydrothermal reaction system, a series of LnCOFs $\mathrm{\small{[Ln_{14}(\mu_{6}{-}O)(\mu_{3}{-}O H)_{20}(I N)_{22}C u_{6}C l_{4}(H_{2}O)_{8}]{\cdot}\mathrm{6H_{2}O}}}$ $\mathrm{{\small~\mathscr{L}n~=~Y,}}$ Gd, Dy)20 containing novel $\mathrm{Ln}_{14}$ clusters were successfully made. In contrast to the frequently encountered isolated tetradecanuclear Ln clusters made up of one octahedron and two vertex-sharing square pyramids, the $\mathrm{Ln}_{14}$ cores consist of one $\left[\mathrm{Ln}_{6}(\mu_{6}{\mathrm{-O}})\right]^{}(\mu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}})_{8}^{\cdot}]^{8+}$ octahedron that shares two opposing apexes with two rare $\scriptstyle\mathrm{[Ln_{5}(}\mu_{3}–\mathrm{OH})_{6}]^{4+}$ trigonal bipyramids. In these LnCOFs, IN ligands exhibit a range of coordination modes from simple to complex (Figure 1): IN ligands in mode I merely stabilize the $\mathrm{Ln}_{14}$ clusters; those in mode II link the nearest $\mathrm{Ln}_{14}$ cores into a pure LnCOF; those in mode III bridge tetrahedral $\mathtt{C u}$ ions and $\mathrm{Ln}_{14}$ cores; and those in mode IV connect dimeric $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Ln}_{14}$ clusters alternately into an LnCOF. Except for the terminal IN ligands of mode I, the other three coordination modes of the IN ligands can construct individual topologies. Nevertheless, the combination of these IN ligands with different coordination modes leads to final stable but condensed frameworks.
These results indicate the effectiveness of induced aggregation of high-nuclearity oxo−hydroxo Ln clusters through IN ligands, even though only several LnCOFs based on low-nuclearity $\left(<10\right)$ oxo−hydroxo Ln clusters have been reported previously. In these high-nuclearity oxo−hydroxo Ln clusters, TM centers and clusters are simultaneously incorporated. The successful construction of such LnCOFs provides an opening into a promising new field of $4\mathrm{f}-3\mathrm{d}$ functional materials and confirms the potential for developing new structural families of LnCOF solid-state materials using nanosized oxo−hydroxo Ln clusters as SBUs.
## 2.2. Synergistic Coordination between Isonicotinic Acid and a Second Ligand
As mentioned above, LnCOFs constructed from nanosized $\mathrm{Ln}_{14}$ clusters, copper ions/clusters, and IN linkers are condensed frameworks because of the four coordination modes of the IN ligands.20 However, making further open frameworks by reducing the coordination modes of the IN ligands is a great challenge. A second ligand can occupy a partial Ln coordination sphere, thereby reducing the number of sites available for the main ligand. After the reduction in the number of coordination modes of the main ligand, a further decrease of the connectivity around each oxo−hydroxo Ln cluster may occur. If a second ligand is introduced, synergistic coordination of the first and second ligands may lead to the transformation of metal clusters and result in new types of LnCOFs. Inspired by the above speculation and on the basis of our previous work, we introduced 1,2-benzenedicarboxylic acid $\left(1,2\mathrm{-}\mathrm{{H}}_{2}\mathrm{{BDC})}$ . As a result, a novel series of sandwich frameworks, $\mathrm{\Delta[Er_{7}(\mu_{3}\mathrm{-}O)(\mu_{3}\mathrm{-}O H)_{6}(1,2\mathrm{-}B D C)](I N)_{9}[C u_{3}X_{4}]}$ 1 $\mathrm{{\cdot}}\mathrm{{{X}}=\mathrm{{Cl},}}$ , Br, denoted $\mathbf{FJ}{-}2\mathbf{a}/\mathbf{b})^{19}$ with the largest oxo−hydroxo Ln wheel cluster to date, were obtained.
To some extent, the use of the second ligand has achieved some part of our proposal. For example, the number of coordination modes of HIN is successfully reduced from four in $\mathrm{Ln}_{14}$ to merely two in the FJ-2 series (Figure 2). In every $\left[\mathrm{Er}_{36}(\mu_{3}{\scriptstyle-}\mathrm{OH})_{30}(\mu_{3}{\scriptstyle-}\mathrm{O})_{6}(\mathrm{IN})_{54}(1,2{\scriptstyle-}\mathrm{BDC})_{6}\right]$ $\left(\operatorname{Er}_{36}\right)$ wheel, there are peripheral $54\mathrm{IN}$ ligands. In detail, two coordination modes of IN ligands exhibit different functions: $36\ \mathrm{IN}$ ligands in $\mu_{3}–\eta^{1}{:}\eta^{1}{:}\overline{{\eta}}^{1}$ fashion serve as linkers joining $\mathrm{Er}_{36}$ wheel clusters and $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{X}_{4}$ $\mathrm{(X\=\Cl,\}$ Br) clusters into extended sandwich frameworks, while the remaining $18\ \mathrm{IN}$ ligands in $\mu_{2}{-}\eta^{1}{:}\eta^{1}$ fashion mainly stabilize the wheel cluster.
Meanwhile, there are also some unexpected results. First and foremost is the transformation of oxo−hydroxo Ln cores. Instead of stabilizing pristine $\mathrm{Ln}_{14}$ clusters, the Ln cores are expanded into larger wheel clusters with diameters of nearly $3.0\ \mathrm{\nm}$ . Second is the transformation of $\mathtt{C u}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ clusters turn into $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{X}_{4}$ clusters. The third one is the highly ordered coordination of the introduced second ligand. Even though there are more than 15 coordination modes, 1,2-BDC exclusively adopts the $\mu_{5}–\eta^{1}{:}\eta^{2}{:}\eta^{1}{:}\eta^{1}$ mode in the LnCOFs.
Although improvement of the porosity of the frameworks has not been achieved well, there is no doubt that 1,2-BDC is an excellent second ligand leading to fascinating LnCOFs. IN ligands induce the aggregation of the $\mathrm{Er}_{36}$ wheel cluster and meanwhile serve as linkers. 1,2-BDC ligands as the second chelating ligands mainly stabilize the wheel cluster and might assist in playing a little inducement role. As a whole, synergistic coordination between IN and 1,2-BDC ligands under hydrothermal conditions results in the formation of these two new sandwich frameworks.19
Apart from synergistic coordination between two types of organic ligand, synergistic coordination between organic and inorganic ligands has also been detected in LnCOFs. In our previous work, the Ln clusters transform from $\mathrm{Ln}_{14}$ cores to $\mathrm{Er}_{36}$ wheels, while the TM clusters change from $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{X}_{2}/\mathrm{Cu}$ to $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{X}_{4}$ ( $\mathbf{\dot{X}}=\mathbf{Br}.$ , Cl) cores. It seems that the introduction of 1,2- BDC has an important influence on expanding the Ln clusters but no significant effect on the growth of TM cluster cores. Thus, the chalcophilic $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ ion with a larger ionic radius than $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ attracted our attention. Its higher coordination numbers and versatile coordination modes were expected to facilitate the formation of larger TM halide clusters. To put this consideration into practice, we synthesized seven LnCOFs, $\big[\mathrm{Ln}_{6}\big(\mu_{3}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{O}\big)_{2}\big]\big(\mathrm{IN}\big)_{18}\big[\bar{\mathrm{Cu}}_{8}\big(\mu_{4}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{I}\big)_{2}\big(\mu_{2}\mathrm{-}\bar{\mathrm{I}}\big)_{3}\big]\cdot\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}$ ( $\mathrm{{\dot{L}n=Y}}$ Nd, Dy, Gd, Sm, Eu, Tb) (FJ-4),22 made up of two distinct layered networks of nanosized $\left[\mathrm{Ln}_{18}(\mu_{3}{\bar{-}}\hat{\mathrm{O}})_{6}(\mathrm{IN})_{48}\right]^{6-}~\left(\mathrm{Ln}_{18}\right)^{-}$ and $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{24}(\mu_{4}\mathrm{-I})_{6}(\mu_{2}\mathrm{-I})_{12}\right]_{6}$ $\left(\mathrm{Cu}_{24}\right)$ wheel clusters (Figure 3). Among these isostructural LnCOFs, the Nd complex exhibits characteristic emission bands in the near-IR region, while the Dy, $\mathrm{{Sm},}$ , and Tb compounds show similar broad fluorescent emission that could be assigned to a combination of iodide to copper charge transfer (LMCT) and $\mathrm{d}{-}s$ transitions by $\mathrm{Cu-}$ Cu interactions within $\mathrm{Cu}_{24}$ clusters.
Obviously, gradual changes in the coordination modes of both the inorganic halide bridges and organic IN ligands can be observed in all of the above-mentioned three examples. In LnCOFs built from $\mathrm{Ln}_{14}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ clusters, Cl ions bridge in a $\mu_{2}$ fashion; in FJ-2, Cl/Br ions bridge in $\mu_{2}$ and $\mu_{3}$ fashion; in FJ-4, I ions take $\mu_{2}$ and $\mu_{4}$ coordination modes. The introduction of large-sized $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ ions with highly connected coordination bridging up to four metal centers is critical for the formation of the $\mathrm{Cu}_{24}$ wheel cluster networks of FJ-4. In contrast to the numbers of coordination modes of IN ligands in $\mathrm{Ln}_{14}$ (four modes)20 and FJ-2 (two modes),19 all of the IN ligands in FJ- $\cdot4^{22}$ only show one coordination mode when the I ions are introduced.
As the $\mathrm{Er}_{36}$ wheel cluster networks in FJ-2 are formed on account of the synergistic coordination between two different organic ligands, the generation of both $\mathrm{Ln}_{18}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}_{24}$ wheel cluster networks in FJ-4 can also be described as resulting from the synergistic coordination between organic and inorganic ligands. Trinuclear $\mathrm{Ln}_{3}(\mu_{3}–0)$ and tetranuclear $\mathrm{Cu}_{4}(\overline{{\mu}}_{4}{-}\mathrm{I})$ cores are two SBUs for the graphite networks of $\mathtt{L n}_{18}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}_{24},$ respectively. Every $\mu_{4}{-}\mathrm{I}$ ion bridges four tetrahedral $\mathtt{C u}$ ions, the coordination spheres of which are completed by $\mu_{2}{-}\mathrm{I}$ ions and $\mathrm{\bfN}_{\mathrm{PY}}$ of IN ligands. On the other hand, each $\mu_{3}–\mathrm{O}$ bridges three Ln ions, the coordination spheres of which are completed by $\mathrm{{{O}_{C O O}}^{-}}$ of IN ligands. Thus, the synergistic coordination between IN and $\mu_{3}–\mathrm{O}$ ligands as well as between IN and $\mu_{4}{-}\mathrm{I}$ ligands results in the formation of two distinct networks of Ln18 and Cu24 wheel clusters.22
Although synergistic coordination between two types of organic ligands as well as between organic and inorganic ligands has been demonstrated, the former is usually noticed in most cases. Besides the 1,2-BDC ligand, acetate is another excellent candidate as a chelating ligand for the following reason: it possesses a carboxylate group on one side and a methyl group with steric hindrance on the opposite side. Thus, it serves as a terminal ligand rather than as a linker. Hereinafter are some examples.
Under hydrothermal conditions, Gu and ${\mathrm{Xue}}^{23}$ obtained $\mathrm{Ln}_{4}(\mu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}})_{2}\mathrm{Cu}_{6}\mathrm{I}_{5}(\mathrm{IN})_{8}(\mathrm{OAc})_{3}$ ( $\mathrm{\dot{L}n}=\mathrm{Nd},$ , Pr; ${\mathrm{HOAc}}=$ acetic acid) (Figure 4), in which 2D Ln wheel cluster layers and 1D copper halide cluster chains are linked by IN ligands to generate eventual LnCOFs. All of the IN ligands exclusively adopt the $\mu_{3}–\eta^{1}{:}\eta^{1}{:}\eta^{1}$ bridging coordination mode, providing the possibility to produce extended frameworks. Chelating OAc ligands show two different coordination modes, $\scriptstyle\mu_{3}-\eta^{2}:\eta^{\breve{2}}$ and $\mu_{2}{-}\eta^{2}{:}\eta^{1},$ , which exhibit high affinity toward Ln ions. As a result, the synergistic coordination between IN and OAc ligands leads to the formation of an interesting nanosized $\mathrm{Ln}_{16}$ wheel cluster with an eight-membered ring of dimensions $12.59\mathrm{~\AA~}\times9.13\mathrm{~\AA~}$
Alternatively, the synergistic coordination between IN and OAc with the support of carbonate and bromide templates leads to the generation of an unprecedented LnCOF based on the drumlike $\mathrm{\bar{[Ho_{48}(I N)_{46}(\mu_{3}\mathrm{-O\hat{H})_{84}(\mu_{4}\mathrm{-OH)_{4}(\mu_{5}\mathrm{-O)_{2}(O A c)_{4}\mathrm{-}}}}}}}$ $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{14}(\mathrm{CO}_{3})\mathrm{Br}_{2}]^{2-}$ $\left(\operatorname{Ho}_{48}\right)$ .24 It is believed that this drumlike cluster represents the highest-nuclearity oxo− hydroxo Ln cluster incorporated in LnCOFs. At the top and bottom of the core structure of $\mathrm{Ho}_{48},$ there are six cornersharing cubane-like $\mathrm{Ho}_{4}$ subunits, while six square-pyramidal $\mathrm{Ho}_{5}$ clusters surround the equatorial ring of the $\mathrm{Ho}_{48}$ core via six corner-sharing Ho atoms to form the barrel of the drum. The surface of the $\mathrm{Ho}_{48}$ cluster is further stabilized by OAc second ligands. In addition, the existence of the small OAc ligand reduces the steric restriction of the huge drumlike cluster (Figure 5). The open $\mathrm{Ho}_{48}$ cluster contains one in situproduced $\bar{\mathrm{CO}}_{3}^{2-}$ ion in the middle and two additional $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ ions in strict symmetric distribution above and below the $\mathrm{CO}_{3}{}^{2-}$ .
The above-mentioned $\mathrm{CO}_{3}{}^{2-}$ is not only a counterion but also functions as a template. In reality, this triangular ligand possesses three coordination sites but with small steric hindrance. Hence, similar small multidentate ligands permit more Ln ions to be incorporated into the cluster, allowing the construction of high-nuclearity Ln clusters.25 Except as a discrete charge balancer in $\mathrm{Zn_{1.5}D y_{26}(I N)_{25}(O A c)_{8}(\dot{C}O_{3})_{11}}\mathrm{-}$ $\mathrm{(OH)}_{26}\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)_{29}$ (Figure 6),26 as many as nine $\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{2-}$ groups play a very important role in constructing the spherical $\mathrm{Dy}_{26}$ cluster: OAc groups make the $\mathrm{Dy}_{26}$ cluster stable and reduce the steric restriction, while IN ligands stabilize the cluster and link the nanosized clusters and the tetrahedral/octahedral $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ centers. It is demonstrated that replacing a fraction of the bigger ligands by smaller ones can reduce the steric restriction and make it possible to obtain high-dimensional LnCOFs.
Aside from chelating ligands like 1,2-BDC and OAc, bridging linkers can also be included in the synergistic coordination. The LnCOF $\mathrm{Er}_{4}\mathrm{(OH)}_{4}\mathrm{Cu}_{5}\mathrm{I}_{4}\mathrm{(IN)}_{6}\mathrm{(NA)}\mathrm{\bar{(}}2,5\mathrm{-}$ pdc) $0.3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}^{27}$ $\mathbf{\check{H}N A}\ =$ nicotinic acid; 2,5-pdc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}2,5\mathbf{\Sigma}$ - pyridinedicarboxylic acid) (Figure 7) is the result of the synergistic coordination of IN, NA, and 2,5-pdc. The main IN ligands induce the aggregation of Ln ions and link the hydroxo Ln clusters with copper halide motifs, while NA ligands play an important role in stabilizing the framework. It is worth noting that the presence of a small amount of NA ligands results from the partial decarboxylation occurring at the ortho position of 2,5-pdc under hydrothermal conditions. Compared with chelating ligands like 1,2-BDC and OAc, the bridging 2,5- pdc linkers contribute additionally to the connection of the frameworks. Herein $^{2,5}$ -pdc with two opposite carboxyl groups links neighboring 1D $\left[\bar{\mathrm{Ln}_{4}}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}\right]_{n}^{8n+}$ chains into 2D layers in the ab plane.
