## 2.3. Induced Aggregation by 4-(4-Pyridyl)benzoic Acid
More than just in dimension, HPBA is more flexible than HIN because its pyridine ring is not coplanar with the benzene ring. Nevertheless, its relatively poor solubility in water and weak coordination ability make using it a challenge task. After much persistence and hard work, we achieved some encouraging results. Under the inducement of HPBA ligands, tetrahedral $\mathrm{Ln}_{4}$ clusters and supertetrahedral $\mathrm{T}_{3}\mathrm{-}\bigl[\mathrm{Cu}_{10}\mathrm{\overline{{I}}}_{8}\bigr]$ clusters were simultaneously incorporated in two supertetrahedral LnCOFs: $2\mathrm{(Ln_{4}C u_{10}I_{8}(\dot{P B A})_{18}){\cdot}8H_{3}O{\cdot}9H_{2}O}$ $\left(\mathrm{Ln}\ =\ \mathrm{Sm}\right.$ , Gd).28 As and six $\mu_{3}–\mathrm{PBA}$ ligands. The 18 PBA ligands are equally divided into six groups. Each group bridges edges of neighboring $\mathrm{Ln}_{4}$ tetrahedra and $\mathrm{Cu}_{10}$ supertetrahedra. Thus, the edge-to-edge connectivity between two types of tetrahedral building units generates six-connected pcu $\alpha$ -Po LnCOFs. In addition, AC impedance measurements revealed its high proton conductivity due to the presence of protonated and lattice water molecules around the hydrophilic $\mathrm{Ln}_{4}$ tetrahedra.
Although $_\mathrm{Ln-N}$ bonds are rarer than Ln−O bonds, their appearance in LnCOFs has already been verified.24,27,29 This indicates the potential to generate pure LnCOFs under ligand inducement. Fortunately, we herein discovered one case of this situation during the research on the HPBA system, formulated as $[\mathrm{Ln}_{7}(\mu_{3}\mathrm{-OH})_{8}(\mathrm{PBA})_{9}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}]\cdot4\mathrm{ClO}_{4}$ ·3HPBA $n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{{Zn}=}$ Y, La, Gd, Dy, Er, Yb) (Figure 9).30 Every $\mathrm{Ln}_{7}$ SBU is surrounded by 15 protonated PBA ligands with two coordination modes: three PBA ligands coordinate in a $\mu_{2}{-}\eta^{1}{:}\eta^{1}$ fashion to join two trigonal bases, further consolidating the trigonal-prismatic clusters and preventing stagger to form the general trigonal-antiprismatic configuration; the other 12 PBA ligands in $\check{\mu}_{3}–\eta^{1}{:}\eta^{1}{:}\eta^{1}$ coordination mode involving the coordination of $_\mathrm{Ln-N}$ bonds further contribute to the extended LnCOFs.
## 2.4. Synergistic Coordination between 4-(4-Pyridyl)benzoic Acid and a Second Ligand
Besides the chelating OAc ligand, the bridging oxalate (ox) ligand was also employed in the lengthened system. It can be seen that small OAc and ox anions play different roles in the formation of LnCOFs. OAc mainly has sustaining and steadying effects, while ox performs the function of connection. The charm of the OAc and ox anions lies not only in their ability to serve as good electron donors but also in their versatile bridging abilities and strong coordination tendencies with metals. As continuous work, we made four series of $\operatorname{LnCOFs}$ : $[\operatorname{La}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\scriptstyle-\mathrm{OH}})_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}(\mathrm{PBA})_{12}\operatorname{Cu}_{11}(\mu_{3}{\scriptstyle-\mathrm{X}})_{6}(\mu_{2}{\scriptstyle-\mathrm{X}})_{3}]$ · ${\mathrm{8H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{'X=Br}$ Cl, FJ-21a/b); $\mathrm{\small{[Ln_{4}(O A c)_{3}(H_{2}O)_{4}(P B A)_{9}]}}.$ - $\big[\mathrm{Cu}\big(\mu_{2}\mathrm{-I}\big)\big](\varpi\big[\mathrm{Cu}_{10}\big(\mu_{3}\mathrm{-I}\big)\big(\mu_{4}\mathrm{-I}\big)_{6}\big(\mu_{5}\mathrm{-I}\big)_{3}\big]\cdot7\mathrm{H}_{2}\:\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{{{Zn}=\mathrm{{Pr}}}}$ , Nd, Sm, Eu, FJ $-22\mathbf{a}/\mathbf{b}/\mathbf{c}/\mathbf{d})$ ; $\mathrm{Eu}_{6}\big(\mathrm{OH}\big)_{2}\mathrm{Cu}_{9}\mathrm{I}_{6}\big(\mathrm{PBA}\big)_{12}\big(\mathrm{ox}\big)_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}^{-1}\$ $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}$ (FJ-23); and $\mathrm{Eu}_{6}\mathrm{Cu}_{7}\mathrm{I}_{7}\big(\mathrm{PBA}\big)_{12}\big(\mathrm{OAc}\big)_{6}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (FJ-24).35
In the previous HIN system study, synergistic coordination between the two different organic ligands led to the formation of FJ-2 (Figure 2), while the synergistic coordination of organic and inorganic ligands generated FJ-4 (Figure 3). Herein in FJ-21 and FJ-22, two types of synergistic coordination, between organic PBA and ox/OAc ligands as well as between organic PBA and inorganic $\mathrm{Br/I}$ ligands, were simultaneously observed (Figure 11a). In addition, synergistic coordination between small equatorial ox/OAc ligands and apical PBA leads to the formation of two sandwiched cluster organic frameworks with bnn hexagonal BN topology in FJ-23 and FJ-24.
In these LnCOFs built from wheel clusters, the second ligand seems display selectivity toward the Ln cluster SBUs. In the presence of oxalate, the Ln cluster SBUs tend to be trinuclear $[\mathrm{La}_{3}(\mu_{3}\mathrm{-OH})(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]^{2+}(\mathrm{La}_{3}),$ , as revealed in the FJ-21 and FJ-23 series (Figure 11b). In the case of acetate, however, the Ln cluster SBUs are more likely to be tetranuclear $\left[\mathrm{Eu}_{4}(\mathrm{OAc})_{3}\right]^{9+}~(\mathrm{Ln}_{4}),$ as found in the FJ-22 and FJ-24 series (Figure 11c). However, these SBUs are exclusively organized in a high-symmetry hexagonal/trigonal crystal system. Notably, six $\mathrm{La}_{3}$ clusters are connected edge-to-edge into a $\mathrm{La}_{18}$ wheel cluster in FJ-21 and FJ-23. On the contrary, $\mathrm{Ln}_{4}$ clusters are linked in different ways into distinct wheels. In FJ-22, $\mathrm{Ln}_{4}$ clusters are connected edge-to-edge to form $\mathrm{Ln}_{24}$ wheel clusters, while in FJ-24, they are bridged in vertex-sharing fashion to give $\mathrm{Ln}_{18}$ wheel clusters.
## 3. INDUCED AGGREGATION AND SYNERGISTIC COORDINATION BY NONLINEAR LIGANDS
Apart from linear ligands, the guidance of induced aggregation and synergistic coordination can also be applied to nonlinear ligands. As analogues of bidirectional organic ligands, nonlinear ligands possess different curvatures from linear ligands. For example, nicotinic acid (HNA) possesses a special coordination angle of $120^{\circ}.$ , compared with $180^{\circ}$ in HIN (Scheme 2).
Similarly, they demonstrate excellent performance in inducing aggregation and synergistic coordination to form LnCOFs on the basis of high nuclearity oxo−hydroxo Ln clusters. As described above, small ligands like $\mathrm{CO}_{3},\mathrm{NO}_{3},$ ox, and OAc are confirmed to be great auxiliary and supporting ligands in cooperation with linear ligands. In the nonlinear ligand systems, such examples can also be detected.36−40 For a quick look at this point, only one representative example is given.
Despite its absence in the final product, the azide anion plays an important role in the formation of the above $\mathrm{NO}_{3}@\mathrm{Gd}_{26}.$ based LnCOF. When the amount of azide is increased and the Ln source is changed from $\operatorname{Gd}(\operatorname{NO}_{3})_{3}$ to $\mathrm{GdCl}_{3}$ , $\left[\mathrm{Ln}_{36}(\mathrm{NA})_{36}-\right.$ $\mathrm{(OH)_{49}(O)_{6}(N O_{3})_{6}(N_{3})_{3}(H_{2}O)_{20}]C l_{2}\cdot28H_{2}O^{40}}$ $\begin{array}{r l r}{\left(\mathrm{Ln}\ \right)}&{{}=}&{\mathrm{Gd},}\end{array}$ , Dy) is obtained (Figure 12). The ${\mathrm{N_{3}}}^{-}$ anions act as end-on ligands bridging the $\mathrm{Ln}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{Ln}_{24}$ clusters. The six pentagonal windows of the huge $\mathrm{Ln}_{36}$ cluster are occupied by six ${\bar{\bf N}}{\bf O}_{3}^{-}$ groups. Similar to the incorporation of one iodine ion into the $\mathrm{Dy}_{26}$ cluster center,25 two $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ anions are captured in the $\mathrm{Ln}_{36}$ sphere. Among the 36 NA ligands, 28 are terminal and eight are in the $\mu_{3}–\eta^{1}{:}\eta^{1}{:}\eta^{1}$ coordination mode. The synergistic coordination of NA ligands and inorganic $\mathrm{NO}_{3},$ /azido ligands affords a square LnCOF based on $\mathrm{Ln}_{36}$ clusters. As a result, large magnetocaloric effects and slow relaxation of magnetization were found in the Gd and Dy compounds, respectively.
## 4. SUMMARY AND PROSPECTS
Induced aggregation and synergistic coordination play important roles in the construction of LnCOFs. As teamwork has promoted development throughout human history, this strategy has facilitated the blossoming of LnCOFs in coordination chemistry. In this Account, we have taken linear or nonlinear ligands as probes and demonstrated specific examples of induced aggregation and synergistic coordination. Besides pure LnCOFs, one developed class includes heterometallic frameworks with the simultaneous involvement of TM clusters, especially copper halide clusters. With respect to the ligand synergistic coordination, not only that between two types of organic ligands but also that between organic and inorganic ligands as well as that based on both simultaneously are extensively observed. In reality, other kinds of ligands, including carboxylic acid ligands like pyrazinecarboxylic acid, pyridinecarboxylic acid, and imidazolecarboxylic acid, can be applied.41,42 For example, the highly porous titanium(IV) dicarboxylate crystalline material MIL-125 has been reported.42
Although a range of structural types of LnCOFs have been explored, this is only the beginning of a very exciting journey. Until now the synthetic approach used most has been the onepot method. In view of the abundant reservation of the discrete oxo−hydroxo Ln cluster precursors, stepwise synthesis would be another effective way to obtain LnCOFs. Moreover, the combined diversity of oxo−hydroxo Ln clusters and organic ligands suggests great opportunities for designing varied structures. In additional to general characteristic properties of magnetism and fluorescence for Ln clusters, the incorporation of linkers in LnCOFs not only enhances the stabilities but also will potentially endow properties relevant to porosity. The budding study of LnCOFs has opened a particular vigorous new area, not merely for pursuing aesthetics but more importantly for creating multifunctional materials with unique properties and useful applications.
Notably, the strategy of induced aggregation and synergistic coordination not only can be used in LnCOFs but also can be applied in polyoxometalate (POM) cluster organic molecular cages, Ln germanate cluster organic frameworks, titanium cluster organic frameworks, etc.11,43−45 For example, the axial tris(hydroxymethyl)aminomethane ligands are grafted onto the surface of a $\mathbf{Ni}_{6}$ -substituted POM cluster organic molecular cage,44 while three peripheral 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate ligands are coordinated to the edge of the triangular $\mathrm{Ni}_{6}$ core and serve as three edges of the cubic cage. Therefore, the synergistic coordination of these two organic ligands plays an important role in the formation of this POM cluster organic molecular cage. One more example is Ln germanate cluster clusters.11 In addition, the synergistic coordination st1r1ateg41y52 organic frameworks of FJ-20 constructed from $\mathrm{Ln}_{11}\mathrm{Ge}_{12}$ can also be extended to titanium cluster organic frameworks. The $\{\mathrm{Ti}_{6}\mathrm{P}_{2}\}$ cluster has a robust phosphonate-stabilized core with labile surface sites.46 When HIN and HNA ligands are introduced, the $\mathbf{N}$ donors from its two outer faces successfully coordinate to diverse copper halide clusters to form titanium organic clusters ranging from 1D chains to 3D frameworks.
By this topical Account, we wish to share our excitement about the induced aggregation and synergistic coordination strategy in building fascinating COFs and offer our own small push to help move forward this beautiful chemistry.
